output:
 First, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.
Using the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(f(x), g(x)) &= \text{GCD}(12x+7, 5x+2) \\
&= \text{GCD}(12x+7 - 2(5x+2), 5x+2) \\
&= \text{GCD}(12x+7 - 10x - 4, 5x+2) \\
&= \text{GCD}(2x+3, 5x+2).
\end{align*}
Again using the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(2x+3, 5x+2) &= \text{GCD}(2x+3 - (5x+2), 5x+2) \\
&= \text{GCD}(2x+3 - 5x - 2, 5x+2) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 5x+2).
\end{align*}
Continuing with the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(-3x+1, 5x+2) &= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 5x+2 - (-3x+1)(1)) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 5x+2 + 3x - 1) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 8x+1).
\end{align*}
Again using the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(-3x+1, 8x+1) &= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 8x+1 - (-3x+1)(-1)) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 8x+1 + 3x - 1) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 11x).
\end{align*}
Continuing with the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(-3x+1, 11x) &= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 11x - (-3x+1)(-1)) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 11x + 3x - 1) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 14x-1).
\end{align*}
Again using the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(-3x+1, 14x-1) &= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 14x-1 - (-3x+1)(-1)) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 14x-1 + 3x - 1) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 17x-2).
\end{align*}
Continuing with the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(-3x+1, 17x-2) &= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 17x-2 - (-3x+1)(-1)) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 17x-2 + 3x - 1) \\
&= \text{GCD}(-3x+1, 20x-3).
\end{align*}
Again using the Euclidean algorithm, we have:
